.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt NQPROVE 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqprove ,
.Nm nqchkp
.Nd prove some files of a capsule, and check such a proof
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fo nqprove
.Fa "const char *dir"
.Fa "const char *const *paths" "size_t np"
.Fa "char *out" "size_t cap" "size_t *outlen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqchkp
.Fa "const char *proof" "size_t n"
.Fa "const nqcopt *o"
.Fa "const char *dir"
.Fa "nqrep **rep"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nqprove
writes to
.Fa out ,
of
.Fa cap
bytes, a proof that the files
.Fa paths Ns Bq 0..np
are in the format 2
.Pq Ql noqtis-proof-1
or format 3
.Pq Ql noqtis-proof-2 , Xr noqtis-capsule 5
capsule in
.Fa dir :
the seal, the manifest's head and, per file, its entry (in format 3,
with its salt), its index and its Merkle audit path (RFC 9162).
The proof holds no name, size or digest of the capsule's other files.
Each path is brought to its normal form, as the manifest lists paths,
and looked up; repeats are proven once, and the entries are in the
order of their indexes.
.Pp
In symbols, with E_i the canonical bytes of entry i of the manifest's
.Ql files
array (n entries), H SHA-256 and
.Sy ||
concatenation, the tree is the Merkle Tree Hash of RFC 9162 section
2.1.1:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
leaf_i      = H(0x00 || E_i)               format 2
leaf_i      = H(0x02 || S_i || E_i)        format 3, S_i the salt
MTH({})     = H("")
MTH({d})    = d
MTH(D[0:n]) = H(0x01 || MTH(D[0:k]) || MTH(D[k:n]))
.Ed
.Pp
with k the largest power of two below n; the root is the head's
.Ql merkle.root .
The proof is
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"entries": [{"entry": E_i, "index": i, "path": [HEX(h_1), ...]}, ...],
 "format": "noqtis-proof-1", "head": HEAD, "seal": SEAL}
.Ed
.Pp
(format 3:
.Ql noqtis-proof-2 ,
each E_i with its salt)
.Pp
where the h are the audit path of RFC 9162 section 2.1.3.1, from the
leaf upward, HEAD the manifest's head (what the seal signs), SEAL the
bytes of
.Pa noqtis.sig ,
and HEX lower-case hex.
.Fa *outlen
gets the proof's size, also when
.Fa cap
is too small, so a call with
.Fa out
.Dv NULL
and
.Fa cap
0 gives the size to allocate.
.Fn nqprove
reads the seal and the manifest with the checker's strict readers but
does not check the capsule: call
.Xr nqchkx 3
first.
.Pp
What a proof reveals: the proven entries in full; the head (the number
of files, the root,
.Ql created ,
.Ql inputs
and
.Ql meta ) ;
each proven entry's index, and so the shape of the tree and its place
in it; and the hashes on its audit paths.
Of a format 2 capsule those hashes are of unsalted leaves, so anyone who
can guess another file (its path and content) can compute its leaf and
confirm the guess.
Of a format 3 capsule every leaf holds a secret salt of 32 bytes, and,
SHA-256 taken as a random oracle, no hash on a path lets a holder of a
candidate file confirm that it is, or is not, one of the other files.
.Pp
.Fn nqchkp
checks a proof of
.Fa n
bytes and returns a report as
.Xr nqchk 3
does: the proof's form
.Pq Ql proof.parse , proof.canonical , proof.fields ,
the seal and its signature over the head with
.Fa o Ns -> Ns Fa trust
and its TSA anchors (the other members of
.Fa o
are not used;
.Fa o
may be
.Dv NULL ) ,
and one
.Ql proof.entry
row per entry whose audit path leads to the head's Merkle root.
With
.Fa dir
not
.Dv NULL ,
each proven file there is also checked against its entry
.Pq Ql file
rows, and a
.Ql partial
row says the capsule's other files are not in the proof; with
.Fa dir
.Dv NULL
the
.Ql partial
row says no file was checked.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn nqprove :
.Bl -tag -width NQENOSUP
.It Dv NQOK
The proof was written.
.It Bq Er NQEBUF
.Fa cap
is too small;
.Fa *outlen
is the size needed.
.It Bq Er NQENOSUP
The capsule is format 1.
.It Bq Er NQEIO
A path is not in the manifest, or a file could not be read.
.It Bq Er NQEDEC
The seal or the manifest does not parse, the head's root is not that
of the entries, or a path has no normal form (a path whose normal form
is over 4096 bytes is
.Er NQELIM ) .
.It Bq Er NQELIM
More paths than a manifest may list.
.It Bq Er NQEARG
A
.Dv NULL
argument, or
.Fa np
0, or a
.Dv NULL
path.
.It Bq Er NQEMEM
The allocation hook returned
.Dv NULL .
.El
.Pp
.Fn nqchkp
returns
.Dv NQOK
when every check passed and
.Er NQEVRFY
when one failed, with the report in
.Fa *rep
either way;
.Er NQEARG
and
.Er NQEMEM
return no report.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
const char *p[1] = {"data/in.csv"};
size_t n = 0;
char *b;
if (nqprove("results", p, 1, NULL, 0, &n) == NQEBUF &&
    (b = malloc(n)) != NULL &&
    nqprove("results", p, 1, b, n, &n) == NQOK) {
  nqcopt o = {0};
  nqrep *rep = NULL;
  o.trust = pk;
  o.trustlen = sizeof pk;
  if (nqchkp(b, n, &o, NULL, &rep) == NQOK)
    puts("data/in.csv is in the capsule that key sealed");
  nqrepfree(rep);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-check 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-prove 1 ,
.Xr nqchkx 3 ,
.Xr nqrep 3 ,
.Xr nqseal2 3 ,
.Xr noqtis-capsule 5
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%A B. Laurie
.%A E. Messeri
.%A R. Stradling
.%R RFC 9162
.%T Certificate Transparency Version 2.0
.%D December 2021
.Re
.Pp
RFC 9162 (experimental) obsoletes RFC 6962, whose Merkle Tree Hash and
audit paths (section 2.1) are the same; the code follows RFC 6962.
